\section{Contrast warnings}{

A warning appears when a colour chosen by the developer (light, dark, or a
\code{\link[bslib:bs_theme]{bslib::bs_theme()}} brand colour) has contrast below 3:1 against the page
background (white in light mode, \verb{#212529} in dark mode, or the \code{bslib}
theme's backgrounds). Contrast is checked when \code{\link[=mascot]{mascot()}} builds the UI and
when \code{\link[=update_mascot]{update_mascot()}} applies colours with \verb{theme = }; \code{\link[=mascot_preview]{mascot_preview()}}
collects the results in its contrast table. A character's own colours and
the built-in palette colours never warn, and the colour is always kept.

R warnings appear only when \code{\link[shiny:in_devmode]{shiny::in_devmode()}} is \code{TRUE}, and always in
\code{\link[=mascot_preview]{mascot_preview()}} and the studio. Set \code{options(mascot.contrast_warnings = TRUE)}
or \code{FALSE} to force them on or off everywhere. A non-flag value warns once
per R session and uses the default. Visitors never see R warnings.
Colours from CSS variables (\verb{css =}) are checked in the browser and warn in its console.

These warnings have the class \code{mascot_contrast}. To silence only these
warnings:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{withCallingHandlers(
  mascot("helper", theme = list(rim = "#f5e6c8")),
  mascot_contrast = function(w) invokeRestart("muffleWarning")
)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{suppressWarnings()} also works, but it hides every warning.
}

\section{A \code{bs_theme()} or explicit colours}{

A \code{\link[bslib:bs_theme]{bslib::bs_theme()}} cannot be combined with \code{palette}, \code{light}, \code{dark} or
\code{css}: \code{theme} takes either the theme or a \code{mascot_theme()}, and a
\code{bs_theme()} passed to \code{mascot_theme()} is an error. It fills only the
character's brand roles (the \code{brand} column of \code{\link[=mascot_colour_roles]{mascot_colour_roles()}}: \code{body}
for \code{primary} and \code{rim} for \code{secondary} on \code{"tessa"}), and the roles that
follow them change with them. A character with no brand roles, such as
\code{"neer"}, keeps its own colours. The colour is the same in light and dark
mode. A brand colour that is dark enough on a white page can fail 3:1 on the
dark background. In development mode (or with
\code{options(mascot.contrast_warnings = TRUE)}; see "Contrast warnings") a
warning of class \code{mascot_contrast} then names the slot and the mode, and the
colour is kept. To change it, give those roles for each mode yourself:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{mascot("helper", theme = mascot_theme(
  light = list(body = "#1b3a6b", rim = "#5c6770"),
  dark = list(body = "#9ec5fe", rim = "#adb5bd")
))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The example uses the brand roles of \code{"tessa"}; other characters name theirs in
\code{\link[=mascot_colour_roles]{mascot_colour_roles()}}. Roles you leave out follow the ones you set, as
described above.
}

